new file mode 100644
index 0000000..bed4298
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.crit-loop/round-6/crit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+**First read.** The eye lands on the headline, "Know what is on your shelves before a customer asks". It is now three lines on desktop and widescreen, and its top is level with the top of the "Reorder this week" card. The eye then goes across to the card's five rows of numbers, then back to the two buttons, which now sit side by side. The page reads as a calm, well-made product page for a bookshop stock tool. It feels quieter and more settled than the before image, because the hero no longer runs as a tall, narrow stack. On tablet and phone the page stacks into one column and looks the same as before.
+
+**What it's for.** It gets an independent bookshop owner to start a 30-day trial, by showing that Shelfmark tells them which titles to reorder.
+
+**Verdict.** The headline fix helped. The hero now reads as one promise, and the buttons sit together under it. The biggest thing still in the way has not changed: the card says "Reorder this week" but shows only raw counts, so the reorder decision that the page promises is still missing.
+
+**Finding status**
+- table-lacks-recommendation: open. The card is the same at all four viewports: three columns (Title, On shelf, Sold this week), no column that states the decision, and no line that compares Shelfmark with how a shop works now.
+- headline-breaks-in-phrases: closed. The h1 is 3 lines on desktop and widescreen (measured: lines 3, 17 characters per line, 394 px wide on desktop, 412 px on widescreen). The breaks are the same as on tablet ("Know what is on / your shelves before / a customer asks"). The h1 top (y 144) is level with the card top, and the two buttons now sit on one row.
+- till-not-named: open. "Works with your till. Sales come in from the shop's card reader as they happen." still names no till, at every viewport.
+
+**New problems.**
+- Desktop and widescreen, the row of three feature notes: the wider hero column changed the note widths. They are now 394, 315 and 315 px, where before they were three equal columns. The middle note now wraps to three lines and the others to two. The third note starts at x 901, which lines up with nothing above it. The row reads as uneven where before it read as a set.
+
+**What would move it most**
+1. The reorder card shows stock and sales but not the reorder decision. This is the "Reorder this week" card, at every viewport. The owner reads "tells you which titles to reorder on Monday morning", then sees only On shelf and Sold this week. They have to work out the decision alone, and nothing compares Shelfmark with the till report or spreadsheet they use now. Fix: add a column that states the decision for each title, and a short line that compares Shelfmark with how a shop works now. The maker must supply the column label, its values, and what the comparison says. Defect. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+
+**Smaller things.**
+- The first feature note, "Works with your till", names no till, so an owner cannot tell whether their till works with it. Name the tills Shelfmark works with; the maker must supply the list. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+- The row of feature notes now has unequal column widths. The middle note wraps to three lines, and the third note's left edge lines up with nothing. Make the three note columns equal again, or put the second and third notes on the card's own column lines. The first note can keep the hero column width only if the other two divide the card width evenly. Viewports: desktop, widescreen.
+
+**What's working.**
+- The three-line headline, with its top level with the card top. The promise now reads in one pass, and the hero and card read as one row.
+- The two buttons side by side on desktop and widescreen. The primary action and the secondary action now read as a pair, and the rust fill of "Start a 30-day trial" stays the only strong color on the page.
+- The phone and tablet layouts did not change. They stack in a sensible order: headline, buttons, card, notes.
